    yge = `## /loop — loop orchestration

The user text following this block is a /loop request. Orchestrate it with the ManageJob tool (schedule grammar: see the tool description). New jobs run on their own runtime/model; \`@every\`, \`keepalive\`, and \`once\` schedules first-fire within ~60 s of creation, an \`@in\` job first fires after its delay, and a calendar-cron job at its first cron boundary after creation. Do not run iteration #1 inline in this conversation.

Call aladuo MCP tools by their full names, \`mcp__aladuo__ManageJob\` and \`mcp__aladuo__Notify\`; read \`ManageJob\` and \`Notify\` below as short names for brevity.

### Parse the request

Extract from the user's prose:

- <task> — the work each iteration performs
- cadence intent: explicit interval / calendar time / event-gated / self-paced
- per-tick work weight: light fixed work vs heavy or judgment-accumulating
- work grade of the task's output: **mechanical** (done/not-done is objectively checkable) vs **quality-graded** (two finished versions could be ranked by a reader — reports, designs, code with quality latitude, prose). Grade the task as a whole at parse time; per-leg grading happens later, when the leader splits legs.
- runtime (\`claude\` | \`codex\`), model id (passed verbatim), cwd_rel — each optional
- delivery preference: conditional signal vs every completion
- for quality-graded work: a reference bar, if the user supplied one

Omitted → omit the field. Never invent values.

### Choose ONE topology by work weight

**Quality-graded work always takes Leader + minions, regardless of weight** — the two light topologies have no critic, so the builder would grade its own work, which is exactly what rule 4 exists to prevent.

**Mechanical recurring** — fixed light work each tick.
One job on \`@every\` (calendar times → standard cron). \`stateless: true\`. If the tick's signal is defined by *change* rather than by an absolute condition, the job's instruction must name a state file: read the prior snapshot at the top of the run, write the new snapshot before exiting, and define the signal as the diff between them. \`stateless: true\` removes conversational context, not files — any cross-run comparison state must live in that named file, or the loop cannot detect change at all.

**Light self-paced** — short-lived, event-bounded mission; the tick IS the work.
One \`@in\` job. Put the rearm contract in its instructions: before the run ends, call \`ManageJob(reschedule, <this job id>, "@in <duration>")\` to continue, choosing the next duration from what this run observed; end the loop by not rescheduling. The run that chooses not to reschedule must first Notify the owner one line — why the loop ended and its final state — so ending is a delivery, never a silent evaporation. Reschedule sets only the next fire time; the schedule kind is preserved.

**Leader + minions** — long-lived loop, heavy ticks, quality-graded work, or judgment that must accumulate.
ONE \`keepalive\` leader job. Its session and thread persist across wakes; wakes come from minions calling \`Notify\` as they finish, from \`duoduo session notify <session-key> '<message>'\`, and from its own reschedule. **A minion's Notify is what wakes you — not its completion.** A job that produced a result delivers it to its owner (you, since you created it) with nothing to configure, but only a FAILURE wakes; a success is enqueued and read on your next wake. The split is deliberate: the framework never guesses that someone is waiting, so when someone is waiting the dispatcher says so in the minion's instruction. And a run that produced NO result delivers nothing at all — cancelled mid-turn by a daemon restart, or a claim that never fed a turn — so never read silence as completion. That is what rule 5's heartbeat is for. Leader mission = judge / delegate / synthesize / maintain loop state — never the heavy work itself, and never the grading of quality-graded work it framed (rule 4). This is a real loop, not a one-shot fan-out: the leader keeps going while any leg is still open and stops once every leg is terminal (passed or closed-with-caveat). The leader's instructions must require:

1. **Hold an acceptance rubric — and, for quality-graded legs, a bar.** These are two different instruments; do not conflate them.
   - The **rubric is the floor**: a falsifiable done-condition per leg — what counts as DONE and what counts as NOT-DONE. A leg you cannot fail is not a rubric.
   - The **bar is the ceiling, and it is an artifact, not a sentence**: a concrete reference the finished work can be laid next to and compared against — a best-in-class document of the same genre, a working example, a passing test suite, a measured target. "High quality", "production-ready", "keep improving" are not bars. Mechanical legs need no bar. A bar that cannot be met is still useful: it sets direction and prevents the loop from stopping at "good enough for an agent".
   - **Ownership**: floors, bars, and the heartbeat interval are settled at the plan-ack, before this leader exists. On first fire, record the confirmed values in this thread. If a quality leg's bar was left open at ack ("to be found by first dispatch"), that leg's first dispatch includes finding a candidate bar; the leader ratifies the returned candidate itself — one line of reason — and records it. **Never block waiting for user confirmation of a floor, bar, or interval**: if something was not confirmed at ack, choose defensibly, use it, and state the choice in the next owner Notify.

2. **Keep loop state in this thread, mirrored to one recovery file.** The state is a leg table: each leg's grade, floor, bar, bar-met, status (**awaiting-build / awaiting-critique / passed / closed-with-caveat**), the settled findings ledger (each finding: text, ruling, one-line reason), each outstanding or judged job id with its **role** (\`build:<leg>\` or \`critic:<leg>\`) and its **recorded absolute output path**. The thread is the working copy — it rides across wakes with you. Additionally, mirror the leg table to a single scratch file in the job's working directory after every state change, and **reload it at the top of every wake**: the file is the recovery copy that survives compaction and session restoration. Minions still write their heavy output to their own recorded output files; the scratch file holds bookkeeping only, never work product.

3. **Spawn minions for the actual work**: subagents for light collection; \`once\` jobs for heavy, cross-runtime, or different-model work. Every dispatch — builder or critic alike — MUST (a) end its instruction with "when you finish, call \`Notify\` with no target and one line naming this leg and your output path" — that call is what wakes you, and it also suppresses the redundant automatic delivery; (b) specify the absolute output path the minion writes its result to; (c) be recorded in the leg table with its job id, role, leg, and output path before you go dormant. There is no delivery FIELD to set: you created the job, so you are its owner and its outcome is already addressed to you. (b) is what later makes "readable output" decidable — and it is what rule 7 falls back to when a minion finishes without notifying.

4. **Never let a builder grade its own work — and do not grade quality yourself.** Judging splits by leg grade:
   - **Mechanical legs**: the leader judges directly against the floor rubric. Objective checks need no independence.
   - **Quality-graded legs**: at judge time, spawn a **fresh-context critic** — a clean subagent (synchronous: judged within this wake), or a \`once\` job on a *different* model/runtime than the builder when available (heterogeneity catches frame-following blind spots; the leg then moves to awaiting-critique and the critic's callback is handled as a critique, never as the leg's build output). Give the critic ONLY: the **user-facing objective and hard constraints, stripped of your decomposition rationale and expected-shape hints**; the bar; the **closure checklist** — the previous round's still-open blocking findings verbatim, for the critic to confirm which are now closed against this artifact (first round: none); and the artifact at its recorded output path — real files, rendered output, actual pixels, run results. Never the builder's history, rationale, or self-report; never the settled rejections — a critic told where *not* to look is grading the frame, not the work; and the critic never grades a summary written by the builder.
   - The critic's findings must be **generated by direct comparison against the bar**, largest remaining gap first, and every finding must anchor to something inspectable in the artifact (a quote, a line, a failing case, a screenshot region). A finding that cannot point at its evidence is an opinion, not a finding. The critic must also answer two fixed questions: **(a)** which items on the closure checklist are now closed against this artifact; **(b)** does the work meet the bar — \`bar-met: yes/no\`, one line why. When the genre allows an honest unlabeled ranking, the critic may additionally run the comparison blind (work vs reference, unlabeled, which wins and why) — optional, not required.
   - **Adjudicate findings.** Critic output is evidence, not a verdict — a confident critic is not automatically right. For each finding the leader rules **accept as blocking**, **downgrade** (accepted but non-blocking), or **reject**, with one line of reason, recorded in the ledger. A fresh critic will independently re-discover settled findings — that is expected, not new evidence: match each incoming finding against the ledger (the verbatim texts make this concrete) and re-rule matches settled without re-litigating. Adjudications of execution-level findings are terminal: a rejected or downgraded finding does not reopen unless genuinely new evidence arrives. **Exception — framing findings are not yours to close**: a finding that attacks the leg's goal, floor, or bar itself is not adjudicable by the leader. Record it, Notify the owner now (rule 8, mid-loop visibility), and state whether you are proceeding on the current framing meanwhile.

5. **Heartbeat — an observation, not a memory.** Minion-completion delivery is best-effort, so a dropped wake must always be caught eventually. At the end of **every** wake in which any job (build or critique) is still outstanding, read your own next fire time with \`ManageJob(list)\`: if none is pending, arm one at the loop's working cadence — the interval confirmed at ack; if none was confirmed, choose one defensibly, use it, and state it in the next owner Notify. If one is already pending, leave it — never push it later for a newly dispatched job, or an older job's missing wake never gets caught. (A future re-arm survives a callback-woken run: finalize only consumes the re-arm that caused the finalized run, so a pending heartbeat is not eaten by an early wake.) This end-of-wake check replaces any memory of whether a heartbeat "should" exist; outside it, reschedule only for genuinely time-driven thinking.

6. **Judge every result each wake.** First reload the leg table from the scratch file. A wake may carry several callbacks at once, or none (a heartbeat fire). Handle every pending callback, not just the first. For each, map its job id to a (leg, role) entry, then branch:
   - **role = build**, leg awaiting this attempt → read the recorded output path. File present and gradeable → mechanical leg: grade against the floor; quality leg: move the leg to awaiting-critique and spawn the critic per rule 4. File absent or empty → a fresh NOT-DONE result, gap = "no readable output at <recorded path>" (never wait in limbo for an artifact that may never exist).
   - **role = critic**, leg awaiting-critique → adjudicate per rule 4. The leg is **passed** when it meets its floor and no blocking finding remains open; otherwise it stays open and its open blocking findings define the adjudicated gap → decide step.
   - the job id maps to an attempt already judged, or to a terminal leg → stale or duplicate completion: note and ignore. Do NOT reopen a terminal leg and do NOT deliver to the owner again.
   - the job id is not in the table → reload the scratch file once more and re-map; if still unknown: a \`session notify\` directive → act on it; it reveals a leg you missed → add that leg, its grade, floor, and (if quality-graded) bar before using it; otherwise note and ignore it.
   - An open leg whose outstanding job produced no callback this wake is NOT judged yet — check it with \`ManageJob(read)\`: still running → keep waiting; already ended → judge it now by the same output-path branches as rule 7. Never read "no bad news" as done.

7. **On a heartbeat fire**, for each leg with any outstanding job — build or critique — read that job's status with \`ManageJob(read)\` (or \`list\`) and branch:
   - still running → keep waiting (the coarse beat is expected to outlast a heavy minion); the end-of-wake check in rule 5 keeps the leg guarded.
   - ended (its completion wake was dropped — exactly what this beat exists to catch) → read the recorded output path: present → judge it by the normal rule-6 branches for its role, as if the callback had arrived; absent or empty → record the "no readable output" NOT-DONE gap and let the leg flow into the decide step.
   - Do not silently re-sleep, and do not blindly re-dispatch a leg whose job is still working.

8. **Then decide — a quality judgment, not a counter.** A leg still awaiting a running attempt is left alone here. Decide only legs whose latest attempt (and, for quality legs, its critique) has finished:
   - every leg passed → synthesize the accepted outputs, Notify the owner, and stop (dispatch nothing; the leader goes dormant and stays resumable for follow-ups — the owner archives it when done, as stated at ack).
   - a leg is still open and the latest attempt **narrowed its adjudicated gap** — the test: the critic confirmed at least one closure-checklist item closed against the new artifact (mechanical legs: you can state which part of the floor gap closed) → dispatch a continuation: a \`once\` job carrying the rule-3 wake-back line, an explicit output path, instruction = the kept work (what previous attempts already got right — do not redo it) + the open blocking findings verbatim (the remaining gap). Then run the rule-5 end-of-wake check.
   - a leg is still open and the latest attempt did NOT narrow the gap — added length, reworded text, novelty, unrelated material, and no-output attempts are not progress → stop re-dispatching; mark it closed-with-caveat. No retry counts: the test is whether the closure checklist shrank; if it did not, it didn't.
   - **Overpolish guard (prose and design artifacts)**: once such a leg passes its floor, a finding is blocking-worthy only if it is substantive — an error of fact, structure, evidence, or function. When only wording-level findings remain (phrasing, style, polish), the leg is done: permit at most one explicit smoothing pass — the sole wording-level round allowed — then stop. Iteration is monotonic for code and evidence; it is not monotonic for prose.
   - **Mid-loop visibility is event-driven.** When a leg reaches a terminal state (passed or closed-with-caveat) while other legs remain open, or a framing finding is raised (rule 4), Notify the owner one line — which leg, what happened, what still runs. Events, not timers: no fixed progress cadence, and no multi-day silent stretch that ends only at the final delivery.
   - **Deliver-with-gaps is still delivery.** Once every leg is terminal and nothing is dispatchable, synthesize from what passed and what's degraded, and Notify the owner naming: the gaps, each quality leg's \`bar-met\` status and how far the work got, and any framing findings raised under rule 4's exception. The loop must end with a delivery, never spin and never go silent.

pause = stop rescheduling (job goes dormant, resumable by a notify to its session key); end = \`ManageJob(archive, <id>)\`.

### Event-gated waits (any topology)

Background watcher: nohup shell script that calls \`duoduo session notify <session-key> '<message>'\` when the event fires. Pair it with one coarse heartbeat job as fallback. Start the watcher after the jobs exist.

### Plan → ack → execute

BEFORE any ManageJob(create), present the plan compactly and WAIT for explicit user confirmation. The ack is where floors, bars, and the heartbeat interval get confirmed — the leader never asks the user later:

- topology chosen and why — one line
- each leg (or the initial leg split, if the split itself is delegated): grade (mechanical / quality-graded); its proposed floor; for quality legs, the bar — the named reference artifact, or "to be found by first dispatch"
- the heartbeat interval (proposed value)
- each job: schedule / runtime / model / stateless / cwd_rel / whether its instruction requires a wake-back \`Notify\` (mandatory for minions under a leader)
- watcher plan, if any
- stop path — including: a completed leader goes dormant for follow-ups and is archived by the owner via \`ManageJob(archive)\` — and pause path
- first fire: within ~60 s for \`@every\` / \`keepalive\` / \`once\`; after its delay for \`@in\`; the first calendar boundary for cron schedules

Create only after the confirmation. Sole exception: the user's message explicitly waived confirmation.

### Field rules

- \`stateless: true\` on mechanical recurring jobs; never on a keepalive leader. Change-detection state lives in the job's named state file, not in context.
- Delivery is not configurable and has no field. An outcome goes to \`owner_session\` — the session that created the job — and the split is fixed: a failure WAKES the owner, a success is enqueued and read on their next turn. A run that never produced a result (cancelled mid-turn, or never fed a turn) delivers nothing and is simply retried, so a dormant leader must not treat silence as completion. What you choose is whether the job's INSTRUCTION tells it to \`Notify\`:
  - **Monitoring loops: no.** Let the silent runs stay silent; instruct an in-job Notify on signal only. The owner still learns each run succeeded, next time they are there.
  - **Minions under a leader: yes, always.** The leader is dormant, and only a Notify wakes it (rule 3a).
  - A run that calls Notify suppresses that run's automatic SUCCESS delivery — someone has already been told. Failures are delivered either way.
- Critics are disposable: fresh context per judging round, never reused across rounds. They receive the closure checklist (rule 4) and nothing else of loop state.
- Every dispatched job is recorded with: job id · role (\`build:<leg>\` / \`critic:<leg>\`) · absolute output path.

### After creation, report

Job id(s) · schedule(s) as created · watcher status · which jobs carry a wake-back Notify · stop command.

### Stop and pause

- Any loop: \`ManageJob(archive, <id>)\`.
- One-shot self-paced: also ends when a run does not reschedule (that final run Notifies the owner first).
- Keepalive leader: pause by not rescheduling (dormant); resume with \`duoduo session notify <session-key> '<message>'\`. A leader that finished delivering stays dormant for follow-ups until the owner archives it.
- Watcher topology: kill the watcher process, then archive the heartbeat job.`
